(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-antibiotics-resistance-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antibiotics and Resistance — Quiz 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Streptogramins are distinctive among the protein synthesis inhibitors because they do what?","choices":["Bind the 30S subunit and cause codon misreading","Interfere with transfer RNA attachment","Use two different components that work together to bind the 50S subunit and inhibit protein translation","Inhibit folic acid synthesis because their structure closely resembles that of para-aminobenzoic acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are produced by Streptomyces species and work against Gram-positives. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What did Sir John Scott Burdon-Sanderson observe in 1870?","choices":["That guinea pigs infected with typhoid could be healed with Penicillium","That cultures of anthrax bacilli are inhibited by mould growth","That culture fluid covered with mould did not grow bacteria","That mycophenolic acid from Penicillium was active against Bacillus anthracis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It sits among a series of pre-Fleming observations of the same phenomenon. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"According to the lecture, resistance occurs because of which two things?","choices":["Antibiotics actively mutate bacterial DNA on contact, and the mutations that result then spread through the population by conjugation and transduction","Genetic variation means some members of the population are less susceptible, and the presence of the antibiotic selects for resistant organisms already present","Overuse alone, independent of any genetic factor","Biofilm formation alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The distinction matters: the drug does not create resistance, it selects the organisms that already carry it. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"When two drugs are used together, what are the three possible interactions the lecture names?","choices":["Indifference — no interaction; synergism — the combined effect is greater than either alone; antagonism — the combined effect is less than either alone","Additive effects, in which the two simply sum; multiplicative effects, in which each amplifies the other; and subtractive effects between the agents","Bactericidal, bacteriostatic and neutral effects","Potentiation, inhibition and tolerance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Using a cocktail is one approach to resistant organisms, but antagonism is the risk that makes the choice matter. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What does the National Antimicrobial Resistance Monitoring System for Enteric Bacteria monitor, and through which agencies?","choices":["Only hospital-acquired infections, through the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention alone, with data drawn from inpatient isolates and reported annually to state health departments for regional comparison","Only retail meats, through the Food and Drug Administration","Antibiotic prescribing rates in outpatient clinics","Antimicrobial resistance of enteric bacteria from humans (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention), retail meats (Food and Drug Administration) and food animals (United States Department of Agriculture), with state and local health departments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is an interagency partnership spanning all three sources. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Who coined the term “antibiotics”, and from which genus did his discoveries come?","choices":["Alexander Fleming; Penicillium","Selman Waksman; Streptomyces","Paul Ehrlich; Treponema","Gerhard Domagk; Penicillium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Waksman discovered over twenty antibiotics from Streptomyces species and won the Nobel prize in 1952. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which drug is correctly paired with its direct toxicity?","choices":["Aminoglycosides — renal and auditory toxicity","Ciprofloxacin — aplastic anaemia","Doxycycline — hyperkalaemia","Tetracycline — renal and hepatic toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The auditory component is what makes this pairing distinctive among the listed toxicities. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Chemotherapy is defined in this lecture as which of the following?","choices":["The treatment of malignancy with cytotoxic agents","The use of drugs to treat a disease","The use of microbial products to stimulate host immunity","The combination of two or more drugs to overcome resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antimicrobial drugs are then the subset that interferes with microbial growth within a host. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Isoniazid and ethambutol both act on mycobacterial cell walls. How do they differ?","choices":["Isoniazid binds the 30S subunit; ethambutol binds the 50S","Isoniazid inhibits DNA gyrase; ethambutol inhibits bacterial RNA polymerase","Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid synthesis; ethambutol inhibits its incorporation","Isoniazid is topical; ethambutol is parenteral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacteria have different cell walls, which is why they need their own agents. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Bacitracin works by which mechanism, and why is it topical only?","choices":["It interferes with bactoprenol, the membrane transporter carrying peptidoglycan monomers to the growing wall, preventing dephosphorylation so no new monomers are inserted; it is too toxic for parenteral use","Amycolatopsis orientalis; a polyene that binds ergosterol in the fungal membrane and forms pores through it, causing leakage of cell contents, which is why it is too toxic for routine systemic use","It inhibits DNA gyrase; it is restricted to urinary tract use","It binds the 30S subunit and causes misreading; it is nephrotoxic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The wall weakens and the bacterium bursts from osmotic lysis. It is produced by Bacillus subtilis and used against Gram-positives. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Cephalosporins were originally isolated from which organism, and how does their generation ordering work?","choices":["Streptomyces griseus; first generation broad spectrum and Gram-negative, second narrowed to Gram-positive, third oral, fourth injected against pseudomonads","Penicillium chrysogenum; all generations are equivalent in spectrum","Bacillus subtilis; later generations are topical only","Cephalosporium acremonium; first generation narrow spectrum and Gram-positive, second extended to include Gram-negative, third including pseudomonads and injected, fourth oral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Their mechanism is similar to the transpeptidase activity of penicillin. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the ESKAPE group refer to, and why does it matter clinically?","choices":["Clinically relevant biofilm producers — Enterococcus faecium, Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Acinetobacter baumannii, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Enterobacter species — which colonise medical devices and host tissues, promoting persistent infection","The six antibiotic classes most often rendered useless by beta-lactamase enzymes — penicillins, cephalosporins, monobactams, carbapenems, glycopeptides and macrolides — which together account for most treatment failure in hospital-acquired infection worldwide","A monitoring programme for resistance in enteric bacteria","The six genetic mechanisms by which resistance develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The devices named are urinary catheters, ventilators, prosthetic joints and cardiac implants; the tissues are chronic wounds and the lungs of cystic fibrosis patients. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the practical consequence of drug clearance for the dosage schedule?","choices":["Doses should be spaced as widely apart as the patient will tolerate, since the drug persists in tissue long after the plasma level falls","Clearance affects only the loading dose, not the interval","You must not let the concentration fall below the minimal inhibitory concentration — and it takes time for the drug to enter the system in the first place","Clearance determines the spectrum of activity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter: the trough must stay above the minimal inhibitory concentration, and onset is not instantaneous. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does tetracycline cause tooth discoloration?","choices":["It stains enamel on contact during oral administration","It binds with calcium during tooth development","It inhibits enamel-forming protein synthesis via the 30S subunit","It causes chronic gingival inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why the timing of exposure, during development, is what matters. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is the therapeutic window?","choices":["The interval between doses at which plasma concentration falls below the minimal inhibitory concentration","The period during which a drug remains detectable in serum","The range of plasma concentrations spanning the minimum concentration for clinical efficacy and toxicity","The range of organisms against which a drug is effective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The therapeutic index is then the calculation used to assess safety and efficacy against that window. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do antisense agents work, and what example is given?","choices":["They bind bacterial ribosomes to halt translation; example, nisin","They deliver bacteriophages to lyse the pathogen; example, phage therapy","They are complementary DNA that binds a pathogen's virulence genes and prevents transcription; example, fomivirsen for cytomegalovirus retinitis","They sequester the iron that a pathogen requires for its metabolism; example, diiodohydroxyquinoline used in amoebic disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Targeting virulence genes rather than the organism itself is what makes the approach distinctive. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which pairing of aminoglycoside to producing organism is correct?","choices":["Streptomycin from Streptomyces griseus; neomycin from Streptomyces fradiae; gentamycin from Micromonospora purpurea","Streptomycin from Streptomyces fradiae; neomycin from Micromonospora purpurea; gentamycin from Streptomyces griseus","All three are produced by Streptomyces griseus","All three are produced synthetically from coal tar dyes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Note that gentamycin's producer is a Micromonospora rather than a Streptomyces. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Minimal bactericidal concentration is defined as the minimum level that does what?","choices":["Kills 99.9% of test organisms","Inhibits the growth of an organism","Reaches the target site in the body","Produces a measurable zone of inhibition on disk diffusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Minimal inhibitory concentration, by contrast, is the minimum level that inhibits growth. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which organisms does the lecture name as typically involved in superinfection?","choices":["Streptococcus pyogenes, Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Moraxella catarrhalis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Treponema pallidum","Escherichia coli and Salmonella enterica","Candida albicans, Clostridioides difficile, hepatitis C virus, human immunodeficiency virus, Aspergillus and resistant staphylococci"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The mix of fungi, bacteria and viruses is the point — reduced normal flora opens the door broadly. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the effect of penicillin's action on penicillin-binding proteins?","choices":["It prevents peptidoglycan monomers from being transported across the cell membrane","It inactivates proteins on the cell membrane involved in cell wall synthesis and maintenance","It binds NAG and NAM subunits so they cannot be incorporated","It prevents dephosphorylation of the membrane transporter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inhibition of transpeptidase then blocks formation of the peptide cross links specifically. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which of these does the lecture list as a behaviour that increases resistance?","choices":["Completing a prescribed regimen in full","Using antibiotics for the common cold and other inappropriate conditions","Using a narrow spectrum agent once the causative organism is known","Susceptibility testing before prescribing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The list also includes misuse or overuse, using outdated or weakened antibiotics, failing to complete a regimen, and using someone else's leftover prescription. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Polymyxin B acts how, and how is it typically used?","choices":["Inhibiting bacterial RNA polymerase and so preventing transcription; used chiefly against tuberculosis and other mycobacterial infections, always in combination with a second agent to limit resistance","It blocks beta-glucan synthesis; given intravenously","It inhibits squalene epoxidase; applied to skin","It disrupts both the outer membrane of Gram-negative cells and the inner membrane by attaching to lipid components; used topically, combined with bacitracin and neomycin in over-the-counter preparations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is produced by Paenibacillus polymyxa. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How do integrase inhibitors work?","choices":["They prevent the viral genes from entering the host chromosome, so the viral DNA is ultimately degraded","They block the proteolytic cleavage of viral polypeptide precursors into mature proteins","They prevent the virus escaping the host cell to infect others","They inhibit uncoating of the virion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After reverse transcription the integrase forms a stable complex with viral DNA and mediates integration; blocking it leaves the DNA unintegrated. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"The resistance mechanisms are grouped under a heading describing what problem?","choices":["The organism grows faster than the drug can act","The host clears the drug before it can work","The antimicrobial does not get to the active site — by decreased permeability, decreased transport, or increased efflux","The drug is inherently bacteriostatic rather than bactericidal, so it slows the organism without ever clearing it from the host"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those three are grouped together as failures of access, before the separate categories of inactivation, target modification and bypass. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say treatment of eukaryotic pathogens is often more difficult than treatment of bacteria?","choices":["Because eukaryotic pathogens replicate considerably faster than bacteria do, so they outpace the drug before an effective concentration is reached","Because cell structure and some physiology resemble those of the human host, so there is great possibility of toxic side effects","Because eukaryotic pathogens are always intracellular","Because eukaryotic pathogens lack a cell wall to target"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There are also not as many drugs in the pharmacopeia for them, precisely because selective toxicity is harder to achieve. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Aminoglycosides act by which mechanism?","choices":["Binding the 50S subunit and inhibiting peptide bond formation","Interfering with transfer RNA attachment","Preventing amino acid translocation from transfer RNA to the ribosomal binding site","Changing the shape of the 30S subunit so messenger RNA codons are not read accurately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Streptomycin, neomycin and gentamycin are the broad-spectrum examples given. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which expressions does the lecture give for the therapeutic index?","choices":["Renal clearance plus hepatic clearance, divided by total clearance","Minimal inhibitory concentration divided by minimal bactericidal concentration, equivalently the trough over the peak plasma level","Zone of inhibition divided by disk concentration","Maximum tolerated dose over minimum inhibitory concentration, equivalently median toxic dose (TD50) over median effective dose (ED50)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both forms are given, and it is a calculation used to assess the safety and efficacy of a drug. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does the E test (epsilometer test) read, and in what units?","choices":["The zone diameter in millimetres, corrected for the molecular weight and diffusion rate of the agent tested","The therapeutic index, as a ratio","The minimal bactericidal concentration, as a percentage kill","The minimal inhibitory concentration in micrograms per millilitre, at the point where the zone of inhibition meets the strip"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why the lecture calls it “MIC on a stick”. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which of these is described as an antimicrobial peptide isolated from an organism?","choices":["Fomivirsen, from bacteriophage","Magainin, from frogs","Remdesivir, from Streptomyces","Imiquimod, from moths"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The examples given are nisin from lactic acid bacteria, magainin from frogs, and cecropin from moths. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Traces of which antibiotic were found in human skeletal remains from Sudanese Nubia dating to 350-550 AD, and from what source?","choices":["Penicillin, from mouldy bread stored alongside the remains in the burial chambers","Streptomycin, from soil contamination of the remains","Tetracycline, from Nubian beer made with fermented grain containing Streptomyces","Quinine, from cinchona bark traded along the Nile"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The fermented grain carried Streptomyces, which is why the skeletal remains carry tetracycline. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
